<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manikya dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1400–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Manikya dynasty ruled the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a> in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> from about the early 15th century until 1949, one of the longest-lasting royal lines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-asia">South Asia</a>. Its kings claimed descent from the lunar dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition, and their history is told in the chronicle known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmala">Rajmala</a>. They lost the plains to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> but kept the hills, ruled as a princely state under the British and acceded to India in 1949.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manikya-dynasty.jpg" alt="Manikya dynasty" width="256" height="340" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Jolle at Spanish Wikipedia. · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_Arms_of_Tripura_Princely_State.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajmala">Rajmala</a> traces the dynasty to Druhya, a son of Yayati of the Lunar dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition, and lists an unbroken line of 144 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a> kings, probably mythological, before Ratna Fa, said to have become the first Manikya when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> granted him the title. Historians now believe the chronicle mistaken in the genealogy and chronology of the first Manikyas. Coins suggest that the first historical Manikya was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-manikya">Maha Manikya</a>, a Tripuri chief who founded the kingdom in the early 1400s by establishing dominance over neighbouring tribes and took the title Manikya in honour of a victory over Bengal.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>His early successors are said to have conquered territory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a>. Tripura was at its largest in the 16th century under kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dhanya-manikya">Dhanya Manikya</a> and Vijaya Manikya II, when its lands ran from the Garo Hills in the north to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a> in the south. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> kings the Manikyas were long rivals of the Muslim rulers of Bengal, fighting sultans, governors and nawabs, before coming under Mughal suzerainty in the early 17th century. When Mughal power waned the conflict with Bengal broke out again, and they turned to the British for aid.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bir-chandra-manikya">Bir Chandra Manikya</a>, who came to the throne in 1870, reformed his government on the British model, adopted Bengali at court and became a friend of the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rabindranath-tagore">Rabindranath Tagore</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Early in the 20th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripura">Tripura</a> was briefly part of the province of Eastern Bengal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>. In 1947 Maharaja Bir Bikram Kishore, the last Manikya monarch, chose to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The final accession was signed by his widow, Kanchan Prava Devi, on behalf of the young Kirit Bikram Kishore, ending five centuries of Manikya rule over the north-east of the subcontinent.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manikya dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Rajmala traces the dynasty through a long legendary line; the historical <b>Manikya dynasty</b> emerged around 1400, when Maha Manikya ruled the hills east of Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/state/tripura-kingdom">Tripura Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
